t interpretations are rejected by the vast majority of Muslims worldwide. More importantly, point to lived reality: countless Muslims are neighbors, doctors, teachers, friends, and volunteers who contribute positively to society every day. Their faith inspires them to do good. ### 4. Help her separate ill-informed opinions from widespread truth Remind her that the loudest bigots don’t represent the majority. There are millions of non-Muslims who respect Islam and recognize its beauty. You could say: *“I know it’s easy to fixate on the hateful voices, but they’re a tiny minority. Most people see Muslims as fellow human beings. Look at how many interfaith events, friendships, and acts of solidarity happen all the time—those rarely make the headlines, but they’re the real story.”* If it helps, you might share examples of public figures, communities, or historical moments where Muslims were respected and valued (without turning it into a lecture). ### 5. Encourage her to lean on her own sense of self and community Remind her that her own character and the character of the Muslims she knows are the ultimate proof. No stereotype can cancel out the goodness she sees in her family, friends, or herself. Suggest: *“When you’re feeling overwhelmed by other people’s false beliefs, try to ground yourself in what you know is real—the kindness you see in your community, the prayers you say, the values you live by. Their ignorance doesn’t define you; your own actions do.”* If she finds it helpful, she might connect with supportive Muslim networks or engage in acts of service, which can reinforce a sense of purpose and counter negativity. ### 6. Give her practical tools to deal with such comments (if she wants them) Sometimes reassurance comes from feeling prepared. Ask gently if she’d like to practice a calm response she could use if someone says something offensive, or if she’d rather just have you as a safe person to vent to. She might appreciate knowing she can simply say, “That